    {
      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Own a serial selection/voting variant. Replace the O(points*k) insertion path only with a provably equivalent approach: preserve global encounter order as the equal-distance secondary key, retain the existing float-distance semantics, and initially retain qsort-based voting to preserve signed-char and strict-tie behavior. Evaluate bounded heaps/adaptive paths for small k, large k, and k equal to total points. Handle or safely fall back for source-observable NaN/non-finite values and partially filled top-k slots. Produce differential-correctness evidence for equal distances, class-frequency ties, k boundaries, and labels out of input order.\",\"B\":\"Own input/data-layout work while holding selection semantics fixed. Investigate whether scanf and grouped allocations dominate end-to-end time; build a competing buffered, semantics-preserving parser only if it accepts the baseline grammar (including `n_groups`) and preserves float conversion. Flatten points into cache-friendly arrays while retaining label and original index. Since the query arrives last, retain sufficient data or compare a general two-pass-buffer design; do not use approximate decimal conversion or input-specific assumptions. Validate formatting, exponents, whitespace, ties, and exact one-character output against the baseline.\",\"C\":\"Own deterministic multicore evaluation on the observed 4-core ARM64 target. Starting from a correct flat representation, partition contiguous original-index ranges; each worker computes a local top-k with the same distance/index ordering, then merge candidates deterministically. Avoid shared heaps, schedule-dependent ties, altered floating arithmetic, and false sharing. Consider a general serial fallback when thread startup, parsing, allocation, or large local candidate sets outweigh compute savings. Measure full-program speed rather than assuming parallel selection helps.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only independently correct components from A/B/C; do not assume parser, heap, and threading gains compose. First differentially validate candidate combinations against the baseline on broad valid generated cases, especially equal-distance ordering, vote ties, k=1/k=all points, float edge cases, and raw output bytes. Then compare repeated end-to-end timings under the unchanged harness and retain the simplest robust winner, including a general fallback if warranted. Do not rely on unsafe fast-math, changed toolchain/target settings, or test-specific thresholds.\",\"analysis\":\"The only measurement is a correct 2.018312 s baseline on 4-core ARM64 (2.010-2.033 s samples), with no profile; parsing, O(points*k) selection, and allocation/layout are hypotheses rather than established bottlenecks. Preserve current-source behavior as the oracle: its accepted input grammar, squared-float distance evaluation, strict `<` stable handling of equal distances, qsort-derived label tie rule, and no extra output. The current grouped input necessarily precedes the query, so storage/parsing and later evaluation must be optimized without changing semantics. Avoid precision-changing rewrites or fast-math; integration must be measured end-to-end and may be slower than a constituent variant.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"a4ae25d609cf54c1d79b120aeb0c6960426747b0b43a9fb70be95bae84c3798c\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Own a serial selection/voting variant. Replace the O(points*k) insertion path only with a provably equivalent approach: preserve global encounter order as the equal-distance secondary key, retain the existing float-distance semantics, and initially retain qsort-based voting to preserve signed-char and strict-tie behavior. Evaluate bounded heaps/adaptive paths for small k, large k, and k equal to total points. Handle or safely fall back for source-observable NaN/non-finite values and partially filled top-k slots. Produce differential-correctness evidence for equal distances, class-frequency ties, k boundaries, and labels out of input order.\",\"B\":\"Own input/data-layout work while holding selection semantics fixed. Investigate whether scanf and grouped allocations dominate end-to-end time; build a competing buffered, semantics-preserving parser only if it accepts the baseline grammar (including `n_groups`) and preserves float conversion. Flatten points into cache-friendly arrays while retaining label and original index. Since the query arrives last, retain sufficient data or compare a general two-pass-buffer design; do not use approximate decimal conversion or input-specific assumptions. Validate formatting, exponents, whitespace, ties, and exact one-character output against the baseline.\",\"C\":\"Own deterministic multicore evaluation on the observed 4-core ARM64 target. Starting from a correct flat representation, partition contiguous original-index ranges; each worker computes a local top-k with the same distance/index ordering, then merge candidates deterministically. Avoid shared heaps, schedule-dependent ties, altered floating arithmetic, and false sharing. Consider a general serial fallback when thread startup, parsing, allocation, or large local candidate sets outweigh compute savings. Measure full-program speed rather than assuming parallel selection helps.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only independently correct components from A/B/C; do not assume parser, heap, and threading gains compose. First differentially validate candidate combinations against the baseline on broad valid generated cases, especially equal-distance ordering, vote ties, k=1/k=all points, float edge cases, and raw output bytes. Then compare repeated end-to-end timings under the unchanged harness and retain the simplest robust winner, including a general fallback if warranted. Do not rely on unsafe fast-math, changed toolchain/target settings, or test-specific thresholds.\",\"analysis\":\"The only measurement is a correct 2.018312 s baseline on 4-core ARM64 (2.010-2.033 s samples), with no profile; parsing, O(points*k) selection, and allocation/layout are hypotheses rather than established bottlenecks. Preserve current-source behavior as the oracle: its accepted input grammar, squared-float distance evaluation, strict `<` stable handling of equal distances, qsort-derived label tie rule, and no extra output. The current grouped input necessarily precedes the query, so storage/parsing and later evaluation must be optimized without changing semantics. Avoid precision-changing rewrites or fast-math; integration must be measured end-to-end and may be slower than a constituent variant.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-5.6-terra\",\"nonce\":\"9f9cafcabb7544e69f8fd6cb2f3098ce\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"a4ae25d609cf54c1d79b120aeb0c6960426747b0b43a9fb70be95bae84c3798c\",\"session_id\":\"15219a6fd62b4dcb91b395dcd4ae03d5\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"d5b5b3314140861b9ab1c463c08eb32c346a885948b115e8a5918cccfc5444c8\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":10359,\"output_tokens\":8748,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":8137}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-5.6-terra\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":19107,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
